      "reasoning": "Wait - the Forest creature is now 5/4! They must have cast Ranger's Guile on it (+1/+1). But Hunter's Edge should have dealt 6 damage to it (Invoker was 5/4, got +1/+1 counter = 6/5, deals 6 damage). Ranger's Guile gave it hexproof... but Hunter's Edge was already targeting it. Did hexproof fizzle it?\n\
\n\
Actually, looking at the board: Ranger's Guile is in graveyard, and the Forest is still alive at 5/4. That means they responded to Hunter's Edge with Ranger's Guile (giving hexproof), which caused Hunter's Edge to fizzle (target became illegal due to hexproof). Clever!\n\
\n\
But Wildheart Invoker still got its +1/+1 counter somehow? Actually looking again - Invoker is 6/5 with a +1/+1 counter. The counter is placed as part of Hunter's Edge's resolution... but if the spell fizzled, it wouldn't resolve at all. Hmm, maybe the spell has two targets and only partially fizzled?\n\
\n\
Regardless, the Forest creature (5/4 with reach) can block. I need to get 2 damage through.\n\
\n\
Attackers available: Dwynen's Elite (3/3), Elf Warrior Token p48 (2/2), Elf Warrior Token p56 (2/2), Wildheart Invoker (6/5).\n\
\n\
Total power: 3 + 2 + 2 + 6 = 13. The 5/4 Forest blocks one creature. If it blocks Invoker (6/5), 8 damage gets through → lethal. If it blocks anything else, Invoker's 6 gets through → lethal.\n\
\n\
Attack with everything!",
